Recursion
      Break a problem down into smaller, self-similar sub-problems that resolve via a base case and an unwinding call stack.
      LC 273

Backtracking(Order Doesn't Matter)
      Build combinations where subset grouping is what counts; use an index pointer to strictly move forward and prevent duplicate pairs.
      LC 51

Backtracking(Order Matters)
      Build permutations where the specific sequence arrangement counts; use a visited array or bitmask instead 
      of a forward index pointer to pick from all available elements.
      LC 37

Backtracking(Split / Partition)
      Divide a string or array into valid contiguous sub-segments, 
      branching at every valid split index before recursively processing the remaining suffix.
      LC 301

Branch Pruning
      Introduce bounding conditions early inside a recursive backtracking search block to 
      kill dead-end decision paths before they run, preventing TLE.
      LC 473

Recurrence Relation
      The explicit mathematical equation that defines the state of a large problem in terms of its smaller, memoized sub-problems.
      LC 1416

Linear DP
      Transition states sequentially along a simple 1D array where each index depends strictly on a fixed window of preceding indices.
      LC 32

Take or Skip (0/1 Knapsack)
      For each item, branch into two decisions—either "take" it and collect its value while consuming capacity, or "skip" it completely.
      LC 123

Rolling Array (Space Optimization)
      Optimize a 2D grid DP O(M*N) down to two 1D rows O(N) by recognizing that the current step only relies on data 
      from the immediate prior row i-1.
      LC 115

Scalar / Dynamic
      The DP table tracks variable numeric values or optimal costs that fluidly accumulate across decisions.
      LC 174

Monotonic
      The DP transition sequence forces values to strictly increase or decrease, tracking boundaries like the Longest Common Subsequence.
      LC 72

Qualitative
      States don't just track numeric counts; they encode structural attributes, states, or specific boolean criteria.
      LC 10

Fixed
        The size dimensions of the DP table grid remain hardcoded or depend directly on stationary, unshifting problem parameters.
        LC 132

Cyclic
        States naturally loop back or wrap around, requiring you to copy/extend the array or use modular arithmetic 
        to break the loop into a linear sequence.
        LC 213

Bounded (0/1) & Backward Loop
        Items are strictly limited; once an item is picked, you must transition to the next index, forcing a Backward Loop if space-optimizing 
        a 1D array to prevent reusing the same item.
        LC 474

Unbounded & Forward Loop
        Items are infinite; you can repeatedly choose the exact same item at your current state, forcing a Forward Loop to naturally 
        build upon already updated capacities.
        LC 1449

State Machine DP
        Explicitly transition between custom qualitative states (e.g., HasStock, NoStock, Cooldown) at each step of an iteration 
        loop to map conditional rules.
        LC 188

Matrix Chain Multiplication (Interval DP)
        Compute the optimal cost for an entire range by checking every possible midpoint to split the interval into two independent sub-intervals ($O(n^3)$).
        LC 312

Tree DP
        Execute dynamic programming over an explicit graph tree structure, using bottom-up DFS to pass optimal sub-tree states 
        from child nodes up to their parents.
        LC 834

Bitmask DP (State Compression)
        Use an integer's binary bits as a boolean array mask ($O(2^n)$) to track subsets or visited configurations 
        when the input size $N$ is small ($N \le 20$).
        LC 847

Digit DP
        Solve combinatorial counting problems up to a massive ceiling integer $X$ by processing numbers digit-by-digit from left to right while 
        tracking strict boundary constraints.
        LC 233



Forward Loop (i to i + 1): 
        Used when the current state depends on past calculated states, moving forward from 0 to N to build the solution.

Backward Loop (i to i - 1): 
        Used when the current state depends on future sub-problems, winding backward from N down to 0 
        (common in game theory or suffix/decision-tree DP).

Bounded (0/1 Knapsack) implies Backward Loop: 
        Iterate backward through capacities (W to 0) to ensure you calculate the current item's state using values from the previous item row, 
        preventing an item from being picked multiple times.

Unbounded (Infinite Knapsack) implies$ Forward Loop: 
        Iterate forward through capacities (0 to W) intentionally allowing the current calculation to use newly updated values from the same row, 
        enabling an item to be picked infinitely.


